  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nUpper level ridging is building in today ahead of an \napproaching trough moving along the US/Canada border. \nTemperatures tonight into tomorrow will be a little above \naverage with dew points gradually rising as precipitable water \nvalues rise to near 2 inches tomorrow afternoon into the \novernight hours. An area of low pressure moving through Southern\nCanada will swing a cold front into the region in the evening \nto early overnight hours. \n\nAhead of this front, wind shear increases with bulk shear \naround 50 knots. The uncertainty will be CAPE as post sunset, \nthe boundary layer will stabilize quickly. Confidence has \nincreased that the western Finger Lakes may see the front arrive\ntowards sunset where there could still be some surface \ninstability for stronger winds to mix down. SPC has highlighted \nthis area with a slight risk of severe thunderstorms. Dry air \nadvecting in behind the thunderstorms could help with downdraft \nproduction from the dry air entrainment into the core of the \nstorms. If the downdrafts can be strong enough, the could punch \ninto the stable boundary layer even a few hours after sunset so \nSPC's marginal risk has been extended into CNY and parts of the \nTwin Tiers. Confidence is low that the severe risk will make it \nmuch east of the Finger Lakes though given that Most Unstable \nCAPE quickly falls below 500 even in some of the more aggressive\nmodels. Thunderstorms may continue thanks to forced lift along \nthe front but their ability to produce damaging winds at the \nsurface will diminish quickly after dark. \n\nFlash flood risk is also lower in this event despite the 2 inch\nPWATs as the front is pretty progressive and the shear vectors \ndont line up well for persistent training. Rainfall rates will \nstill be high with any thunderstorm so localized urban flooding \nis possible if thunderstorms move through a larger urban area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nThe front is likely east of the region by Thursday morning with\ndrier NW flow. With the front sweeping most moisture well off \nto the southeast and the NW flow continuing through the weekend,\ncooler and dry weather is expected through this weekend. Zonal \nflow aloft will prevent any significant cool downs or warm ups \nand with the lack of moisture in place, any shortwaves moving \nthrough will just cloud things up briefly rather than produce \nmuch precipitation. Sunday into Sunday night has the best chance\nat some isolated to scattered rain showers as models are \nhinting at a stronger shortwave and cold front moving in.",
